FLTextPut("teachings", "upanishads", {"plan":"upanishads","div":"81cb35ca0e","days":[{"d":1,"at":[0],"ref":"Isa Upanishad 1","key":"All this, whatsoever exists in the universe, should be covered by the Lord. Having renounced (the unreal), enjoy (the Real). Do not covet the wealth of any man.","s":"The Isa’s eighteen verses run from renunciation and work to a closing prayer as the body goes to ashes; Śaṅkara, commentary on verse 1, reads the covering as seeing the universe, moving and unmoving, as the Self, from which renunciation follows, and ‘enjoy’ as protect.","by":"Śaṅkara"},{"d":2,"at":[37],"ref":"Katha Upanishad 1.1.20","key":"There is this doubt regarding what becomes of a man after death. Some say he exists, others that he does not exist.","s":"Granted his father’s peace and the heavenly fire, Nachiketas asks Death for the third boon; Śaṅkara (Advaita), commentary on 1.1.20, reads the doubt as whether a self distinct from body, senses, mind and intellect passes into another body, and Madhva (Dvaita), on the same verse, as whether the Lord rules the liberated souls too.","by":"Śaṅkara"},{"d":3,"at":[48],"ref":"Katha Upanishad 1.2.2","key":"The good and the pleasant approach man; the wise examines both and discriminates between them; the wise prefers the good to the pleasant, but the foolish man chooses the pleasant through love of bodily pleasure.","s":"Death begins his teaching, now that Nachiketas has refused every lesser gift, with two aims that bind every person; Śaṅkara, commentary on 1.2.2, reads the two as arriving as if mingled, and the wise as parting them as a flamingo parts milk from water.","by":"Śaṅkara"},{"d":4,"at":[66],"ref":"Katha Upanishad 1.2.20","key":"The Self is subtler than the subtle, greater than the great; It dwells in the heart of each living being. He who is free from desire and free from grief, with mind and senses tranquil, beholds the glory of the Atman.","s":"After calling the Self unborn and never slain, Death turns to how it is seen; Madhva (Dvaita), commentary on 1.2.20, reads the one free from desire as having firm faith in Vishnu, and the glory as the Lord’s superiority to the self.","by":"Madhva"},{"d":5,"at":[85],"ref":"Katha Upanishad 1.3.14","key":"Arise! Awake! Having reached the Great Ones (illumined Teachers), gain understanding.","s":"After the parable of the chariot and the ascent beyond the senses, the section turns to exhortation; Śaṅkara, commentary on 1.3.14, hears scripture calling like a compassionate mother to beings asleep in beginningless ignorance to wake and realise the Self through a teacher.","by":"Śaṅkara"},{"d":6,"at":[103],"ref":"Katha Upanishad 2.1.15","key":"O Gautama (Nachiketas), as pure water poured into pure water becomes one, so also is it with the Self of an illumined Knower (he becomes one with the Supreme).","s":"Closing a section that warns against seeing difference, Death ends with a second image of water; Śaṅkara (Advaita), commentary on 2.1.15, reads the knower’s self as becoming the very same, once the sense of difference is gone, and Madhva (Dvaita), on the same verse, as becoming like the Lord but not identical with Him.","by":"Śaṅkara"},{"d":7,"at":[114],"ref":"Katha Upanishad 2.2.11","key":"As the sun, the eye of the whole world, is not defiled by external impurities seen by the eyes, thus the one inner Self of all living beings is not defiled by the misery of the world, being outside it.","s":"Yama adds the sun to his likenesses of fire and air; Śaṅkara, on 2.2.11, reads the world’s misery as springing from a false notion that ignorance lays on the Self, as a snake on a rope, while the Self, like the rope, stays untainted.","by":"Śaṅkara"},{"d":8,"at":[119],"ref":"Katha Upanishad 2.3.1","key":"This ancient Aswattha tree has its root above and branches below.","s":"Opening the Katha’s last section, Yama pictures an ancient tree and names its source pure, Brahman, deathless; Śaṅkara, on 2.3.1, reads the tree as saṃsāra, changing every moment like a mirage, rooted above in Brahman and felled by the sword of knowing the supreme Self.","by":"Śaṅkara"},{"d":9,"at":[139],"ref":"Kena Upanishad 1.3","key":"It is distinct from the known and also It is beyond the unknown.","s":"Having said that eye, speech and mind cannot reach That, the teacher cites the ancients; Śaṅkara, on 1.3, reads it as saying that only one’s own Self can be other than both known and unknown, and so that the Self is Brahman.","by":"Śaṅkara"},{"d":10,"at":[147],"ref":"Kena Upanishad 2.3","key":"He who thinks he knows It not, knows It. He who thinks he knows It, knows It not.","s":"Following the disciple’s answer, the text sets knowing against not knowing; Śaṅkara, on 2.3, reads it as Brahman being well known to one sure it is unknowable, while those who think they know it confuse the Self with senses, mind and intellect.","by":"Śaṅkara"},{"d":11,"at":[161],"ref":"Kena Upanishad 3.12","key":"Then he saw in that very space a woman beautifully adorned, Uma of golden hue, daughter of Haimavat (Himalaya).","s":"The mystery that baffled Agni and Vayu vanishes before Indra, and a figure appears; Śaṅkara, on 3.12, reads the woman as knowledge, taking Uma’s form because Indra, drawn to the Spirit, stayed pondering it where Agni and Vayu turned back.","by":"Śaṅkara"},{"d":12,"at":[169],"ref":"Kena Upanishad 4.8","key":"The Vedas are its limbs. Truth is its support.","s":"Having declared the Upanishad taught, the teacher names tapas, dama and karma as its footing; Madhva (Dvaita), on 4.8, reads truth here as the Mīmāṃsā, the science of interpretation by which the truth is determined.","by":"Madhva"}]});
